    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210449": {
      "title": "Women&#39;s Health Equity Fellowship (Visiting Instructor)",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Women&#39;s Health Equity Fellowship (Visiting Instructor) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Women's Health Equity Fellowship (Visiting Instructor) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Position/Rank Womens Health Equity Fellowship (Visiting Instructor) Department 00925 - General Obstetrics & Gyn Div City Salt Lake City, UT Track Non Tenure Track New Position to Begin July 2027 Details The University of Utah Women’s Health Equity Fellowship ( WHE ) is an OBGYN departmental fellowship, which aims to address health equity for women in low resource settings. Core competencies in clinical care, research, professionalism and leadership, as well as global health ethics, partnerships and cultural competencies, will provide trainees with a well-rounded and enriching professional and educational experience. The WHE Fellow will spend their first year primarily working at Tsehootsooi Medical Center in Fort Defiance, Arizona, serving the peoples of the Navajo Nation. This will follow a short time spent in Salt Lake City, where the fellow has the option to complete a Master of Science in Clinical Investigation. While on-site, the Fellow will learn from their site mentors and hone their clinical skills. In their second year, trainees will primarily live and train in Kigali, Rwanda with University partners at the University of Rwanda, Kigali ( CHUK ). The Fellow will also spend several months in Utah, allowing them to receive mentorship and work toward completion of their chosen research project, wherein they will re-apply their clinical knowledge and develop competencies in clinical innovation and quality improvement. During that time in Salt Lake City, the fellow will also function as a general ob/gyn, supervising medical students and residents on labor and delivery and the gynecology inpatient service. We are seeking passionate individuals who are drawn to serving local, rural and global communities in low resource settings. One position is available for the Women’s Health Equity Fellowship on a competitive basis, for the July 2026 start date. Eligible applicants must have completed an accredited Obstetrics and Gynecology residency and be licensed to practice medicine in their current home state. They must be Board eligible or Board certified in general obstetrics and gynecology, and capable of serving as a generalist Ob-Gyn attending physician (e.g., a candidate with a long hiatus from providing obstetric care or laparoscopic surgical care would not have adequate skills). Completion of the General Obstetrics and Gynecology ABOG written board exam prior to beginning fellowship is expected except under extenuating circumstances. Application Requirements: • CV/Resume (Please include all past experience with global and rural health) • CREOG Score Report • Personal Statement (limit to 1.5 pages) • Statement detailing applicant’s personal philosophy on health equity and rural health (limit 1 page) • Letters of Recommendation, to be sent separately by author to whefellowship@hsc.utah.edu o If you are a graduating resident, please send one letter from your Program Director and 2 other faculty members. o If you are an attending physician, please send one letter from your current supervising physician and 2 other colleagues who know you well. • International medical graduates must be ECFMG Certified and ABOG eligible. All international (non U.S. residents) must submit copies of the following requirements in addition to those listed above: o Valid ECFGM certificate with clinical skills assessment certification Please submit all materials in one PDF document in the order listed above. Send complete application or any questions to whefellowship@hsc.utah.edu . Upon receipt of the listed application materials, a pdf form will be sent to the applicant to be completed within one week of receiving. Applications will not be considered complete, without this form. Quick link to posting: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/191553 The University of Utah Health (U of U Health) is a patient focused center distinguished by collaboration, excellence, leadership, and respect. The U of U Health values candidates who are committed to fostering and furthering the culture of compassion, collaboration, innovation, accountability, acceptance, integrity, quality, and trust that is integral to our mission. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service, may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 11/04/2025 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN03949F Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). One measure of faculty diversity at the School of Medicine is the proportion of faculty members who come from a rural background. Did you graduate high school from a town with less than 10,000 persons? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210444": {
      "title": "Director of the School of Dance",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Director of the School of Dance Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Director of the School of Dance Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Tenured, Open Department 00070 - College of Fine Arts-Dean City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin July 1, 2027 Details The College of Fine Arts at the University of Utah invites nominations and applications for a visionary leader to serve as the next Director of its School of Dance. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. Reporting to the Dean of the College of Fine Arts, the Director is the administrative head of the School, overseeing its personnel, operations, curriculum, strategic direction, and acting as the School’s representative and advocate within the College, University, and wider community. The School of Dance is known worldwide for its faculty and alumni, and a commitment to excellence in dance education. Versatile teaching, performance, and research are the hallmarks of our community. This is an exciting, transformational moment in the history of the School, as we continue defining and refining what dance education looks like in the twenty-first century. We seek a Director with a comprehensive understanding of the breadth and depth of dance as an enduring, varied, and evolving form of artistic, cultural, and personal practice. The new Director will have a successful record of transparent, collaborative leadership, with a proven ability to work with different groups to sustain the School’s reputation for excellence. Our ideal candidate will have a deep knowledge of the traditions of ballet and contemporary dance, a history of effective advocacy for both, a clear perspective on current discipline-wide trends, and the ability to offer a compelling vision for how the School will meet challenges and continue to thrive as dance educators, creators, performers, researchers, and leaders in the field. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS · Terminal degree in dance, dance studies, or a related field. · A distinguished record of creative or scholarly achievement in dance commensurate with a faculty appointment at the rank of a tenured Associate Professor or Professor, including a body of work that represents a significant contribution to the field; demonstrated excellence in post-secondary teaching; and evidence of an active, ongoing program of creative or scholarly research. · Significant leadership, administrative, and supervisory experience, including strategic and financial planning, curricular innovation, & recruitment and retention of faculty, staff, and students. · Evidence of an open-minded and multifaceted commitment to the art and the people involved in it, with demonstrated ability to lead effectively and foster belonging and community in the School of Dance while supporting students and faculty of various backgrounds, artistic practices and perspectives. RESPONSIBILITIES The Director will (1) guide the efforts of faculty to establish a strategic vision for dance education and research within the context of a Research 1 University; (2) provide collaborative, transparent, and empathetic leadership for the School; (3) sustain and enhance the reputation of the School both nationally and internationally; (4) serve the School in official business with university authorities, students, and the public; (5) create and support professional development initiatives for faculty and staff, and foster, encourage and evaluate faculty research and productivity at the level of R1 expectations; (6) promote student success through recruitment and retention efforts; (7) oversee curricular activities, including ongoing efforts to diversify and modernize the curricula; (8) administer school finances, resources, and facilities; (9) engage in donor relationships and funds development; (10) engage with the National Association of Schools of Dance and maintain accreditation, and (11) be an active participant as a member of the Executive Committee of the College of Fine Arts in furthering the College and University’s goals and mission. The Director of the School of Dance maintains a full administrative workload with no required teaching obligations. About the School of Dance The long heritage of dance at the University of Utah began in 1892 with a program developed by Maude May Babcock. Elizabeth R. Hayes was appointed Director of Modern Dance in 1940. The University of Utah’s Department of Modern Dance became a recognized department across the nation, and eventually was the home, incubator, and launching pad for the founders of both Ririe-Woodbury Dance Company and Repertory Dance Theatre. Willam F. Christensen, a noted dancer, teacher and choreographer who founded San Francisco Ballet, returned to Utah in 1951 and founded one of the first ballet programs at an American university as well as Ballet West in 1963. In 2016, our internationally-acclaimed and NASD -accredited programs in Ballet and Modern Dance united to form the School of Dance—retaining the distinctiveness of each discipline, while creating new opportunities for interdisciplinary exploration and collaboration. The BFA degrees focus largely on performance, creative process, and teaching. The MFA degrees focus on artistic and scholarly development of leaders to create, educate and inspire. The School is comprised of 18 full-time faculty and 13 full-time staff serving over 200 undergraduate majors, 35 undergraduate minors, and 12 graduate students. Studio classes are accompanied by a full complement of live musicians. The University of Utah School of Dance is housed in the 64,000 square foot Alice Sheets Marriott Center for Dance, a beautiful facility designed and constructed specifically for dance. The building features a 333-seat dance theater, six large dance studios, two small dance studios, two academic classrooms, a student lounge, two large dressing/locker rooms, an audiovisual lab for video and sound production, an in-house dance clinic with athletic trainer onsite, a recently renovated cross-training room, a faculty conference room and kitchen, suites of administrative and faculty offices, and in-house costume and scenery shops. The dance studios are full of natural light, with high ceilings and sprung Harlequin flooring, equipped with mirrors, ballet barres, pianos, and updated audio/visual equipment. Additional information about School of Dance facilities can be found here . The School of Dance is one of five academic units in the College of Fine Arts , which also includes the Departments of Art & Art History , Film & Media Arts , and Theatre ; and the School of Music . The school shares collaborative working relationships with the University’s four professional arts affiliates which include Pioneer Theatre Company , Tanner Dance , the Utah Museum of Fine Arts and UtahPresents . Additional information about the School of Dance can be found at https://www.dance.utah.edu . About the University The University of Utah is the state’s public flagship institution of higher education with an enrollment of approximately 38,000 undergraduate and graduate students. A member of the American Association of Universities, the University of Utah is classified by the Carnegie Foundation among the research universities with the “highest research activity” in the nation. The university has been named by Forbes as one of the top ten employers in the state and recognized by U.S. News & World Report as one of the best national universities. The University of Utah has several campus-wide initiatives that contribute to a rich campus climate that fosters student success, attracts academic and professional talent, improves access to higher education, provides unmatched societal impact, and strengthens the U’s ability to educate the next generation of leaders. About Salt Lake City The University is located in Salt Lake City, the center of a metropolitan area with a population of approximately 1.3 million residents. The city is known for extensive arts and cultural activities, boasts a new major international airport, and has received international recognition for its light rail system, foodie culture, and downtown renewal. Salt Lake City’s dance community is a major contributor to the wide-ranging and thriving artistic and cultural landscape of the valley. It encompasses a broad range of dance traditions, communities, and practitioners, including those rooted in Latin American, Native American, Caribbean, and Pacific Islander cultural traditions. The city is also home to a significant concert dance community, represented by long-standing, nationally recognized companies Ballet West, Repertory Dance Theatre, and Ririe-Woodbury Dance Company, whose roots are in the University’s dance programs. The dance community also hosts local companies such as Ballet Las Américas de Utah, and includes several founded by alumni of the School, such as SALT Contemporary Dance, Chrysalis Ballet, Odyssey Dance Theatre, and Utah Metropolitan Ballet. Located at the foot of the Wasatch Mountains, Salt Lake City offers unparalleled opportunities for outdoor recreation, with ten world-class ski resorts and five national parks within hours of the city. Salt Lake City is home to a robust array of diverse communities facilitating numerous opportunities for interaction with a range of civic and political organizations, given the city’s role as the governmental and social nerve center of the state. In 2023, U.S News and World Report ranked Utah as the best state in the U.S.A. and Salt Lake City as one of the top 100 best places to live in the nation. Salt Lake City currently holds a 100/100 rating on the Human Rights Campaign’s Municipal Equality Index for its inclusivity of the LGBTQ+ community. Utah is home to eight federally recognized Native American Tribes and the largest Pacific Islander population outside of Hawaii and Alaska. The city and surrounding area will play host to the 2034 Winter Olympics (a second time for the state). APPLICATION Please submit (1) letter of application, not to exceed three pages, addressing the position’s qualifications and responsibilities, including a statement of philosophy that addresses leadership of a school of dance; (2) curriculum vitae; and (3) names and contact information for five references. The candidate will be notified before references are contacted. Screening of applications will begin November 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled . For additional information, please contact Dr. Kimberly H. Councill, School of Dance Director Search Committee Chair, kim.councill@utah.edu EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates For additional information, please contact Dr. Kimberly H. Councill, School of Dance Director Search Committee Chair, kim.councill@utah.edu Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 03/01/2027 Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01598CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you have a terminal degree in dance, dance studies, or a related field? Yes No Do you have achievement in dance commensurate with a faculty appointment at the rank of a tenured Associate Professor or Professor? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only Resume Applicant Document - Other University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210435": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor (Lecturer) in Systems Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant/Associate/Professor (Lecturer) in Systems Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate/Professor (Lecturer) in Systems Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant/Associate/Professor (Lecturer) Department 00068 - Mechanical Engineering City Salt Lake City, UT Track Non Tenure Track New Position to Begin 01/01/2027 Details The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Utah ( https://mech.utah.edu/ ) invites applications for one career line lecturing position with an anticipated start date in Fall 2027. In 2024, the department started an MS in Systems Engineering and a Masters of Engineering Management both on-campus and online. These programs are in high demand and there are already 100 students matriculated or graduated. To further emphasize the growth, the Fundamentals of Systems Engineering class in Fall 2026 has over 150 enrolled students. The department is planning to offer the Fundamentals of Systems Engineering and the Systems Engineering and Integration courses every semester. Currently these classes are offered once a year and are rated in the top 15% of courses offered by the Price College of Engineering. The successful candidate must be able to teach both of these classes following INCOSE’s guidelines and at a high level of engagement with students. Furthermore, the faculty member will also need to offer these classes effectively to an online audience. Depending upon service, outreach, and other activities, the faculty position is between a 2-2 and 3-3 teaching load per year. There is an option to offer a summer class for additional pay. Thus, the candidate should plan on developing a few quality courses in any Systems, Industrial and Management Engineering ( SIME ) field. Currently, the department only has 5 career line SIME faculty with an average duration of over 6 years at the U. It is important that the individual creates new classes not currently being offered. All courses in these SIME fields are open to be developed and offered. Some current needs are supply chain, logistics, reliability, quality control, information systems, inventory control, etc., but the exact classes are highly adaptable and selectable by the hired individual. The successful candidate will also be encouraged to propose new courses that reflect their own expertise, emerging technologies, evolving industry needs, and the continued growth of the SIME programs. The SIME programs are growing so fast that there is currently a proposal to start a Doctor of Engineering in Systems Engineering and a Doctor of Engineering in Engineering Management. These programs are likely to pass, and the candidate will have an opportunity to advise applied doctoral research with students working in industry. The position has no formal research requirement, but as a tier 1 research institution, research is always encouraged. Applicants shall have a terminal degree, Ph.D., Doctor of Engineering, or Sc.D. The successful applicant should have evidence of quality teaching and the ability to teach quality graduate classes both online and in person. Depending upon the candidates experience, candidates could be considered at the level of associate (Lecturer) or full professor (Lecturer). The University of Utah is research-focused institution that is home to more than 35,000 students, world-class faculty and researchers, a medical campus, and many start-up companies. The Department of Mechanical Engineering has experienced tremendous growth over the past decade, fueled by the State of Utah’s Engineering Initiative, and currently houses more than 60 tenure-line and career-line faculty members, over 1,500 undergraduate and 300 graduate students. The University of Utah campus is situated in Salt Lake City, a growing, increasingly diverse, metropolitan city with a population of 1M nestled against the backdrop of the beautiful Wasatch Mountains. The greater SLC valley is an important economic hub of the mountain west and is home to a number of large technology and fortune 500 companies. SLC residents enjoy a highly accessible and walkable downtown with vibrant restaurants, sports, nightlife, and cultural events. Salt Lake City residents enjoy easy access to national parks (8 within a few hours’ drive), worldclass skiing/snowboarding (7 resorts within 1 hour), hiking, fishing, biking, and kayaking. In addition, faculty members enjoy free access to public transportation and the convenience of an international airport located only 15 minutes from campus. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Review of applications will begin on October 25, 2026. Applications must be submitted electronically and should include: (1) a cover letter highlighting the applicant’s qualifications and specifying which courses the applicant is interested in teaching or developing, (2) current curriculum vitae, (3) teaching statement, (4) evidence of effective teaching including student evaluations from college courses (if available) and (5) contact information for a minimum of three references. Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 11/15/2026 Open Until Filled No Requisition Number PRN01595CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Optional Documents Teaching Evaluations University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210434": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor in Bionics",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant/Associate/Professor in Bionics Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate/Professor in Bionics Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant/Associate/Professor Department 00068 - Mechanical Engineering City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 07/01/2027 Details The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Utah (https://mech.utah.edu/) invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track faculty position in Bionics , with an anticipated start date in the Fall Semester 2027. Candidates with exceptional backgrounds and experience across diverse areas of bionics are strongly encouraged to apply. Relevant areas of interest include: Upper-Extremity Bionics & Manipulation (with an emphasis on prosthetics and orthotics), Invasive Neural Interfaces & Sensory Restoration, Soft Robotics & Smart Materials (for assistance and rehabilitation), Computational Neuromechanics (with an emphasis on neural decoding), Bionic Reconstruction and Tissue-Machine Interfaces, Neuromuscular and Functional Electrical Stimulation ( NMES / FES ), Biohybrid Systems, and other new and emerging areas in Bionics. Although all areas of Bionics will be considered, preference will be given to individuals focused on collaborating with clinical partners within the Spencer Fox Eccles School of Medicine (such as the Craig H. Neilsen Rehabilitation Hospital, the University Orthopaedic Center, and the Departments of Neurology, Neurosurgery, and Plastics) and the nearby George E. Wahlen Department of Veterans Affairs Medical Center. Candidates must have a demonstrated track record of high-quality research, as evidenced by highly regarded scholarly publications, and must exhibit strong potential (junior faculty) or an established record (senior faculty) of securing extramural funding. Successful candidates will be expected to actively contribute to the College’s growing research presence and strategic center-level initiatives in Bionics. Additionally, candidates are expected to teach core undergraduate engineering courses and develop new specialized graduate coursework in bionics-related areas. Applicants must hold an earned Ph.D. or Sc.D. in Engineering, Robotics, or a closely related field. The University of Utah is a research-focused institution home to more than 35,000 students, world-class faculty, and a vibrant start-up ecosystem. A unique advantage of the university is the co-location of the engineering and medical campuses, fostering seamless translational research. The Department of Mechanical Engineering has grown tremendously over the past decade, fueled by the State of Utah’s Engineering Initiative, and now includes more than 45 tenure-line faculty members, over 1,500 undergraduate students, and 250 graduate students. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to engage with established interdisciplinary hubs like the Utah Robotics Center, the Interdepartmental Center for Neural Interfaces, the Neurotechnology Training Program, the Neuroscience Program, and the Rocky Mountain Center for Occupational and Environmental Health. Joint appointments with the Spencer Fox Eccles School of Medicine will be considered as appropriate for each candidate. The University of Utah campus is situated in Salt Lake City, a growing, increasingly diverse metropolitan area with a population of over 1.2 million, nestled against the backdrop of the beautiful Wasatch Mountains. The greater SLC valley is a major economic hub of the Mountain West and home to a rapidly expanding technology sector and numerous Fortune 500 companies. Residents benefit from a highly accessible, walkable downtown featuring vibrant restaurants, professional sports, nightlife, and cultural events. The region also offers unparalleled access to outdoor recreation, including eight national parks within a few hours’ drive, world-class skiing and snowboarding at seven resorts within an hour, and exceptional hiking, fishing, mountain biking, and rafting. In addition, faculty members receive free access to public transportation and the convenience of a major international airport located just 15 minutes from campus. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates All applications received by January 15, 2027 will receive full consideration. Applications must be submitted electronically and should include: a cover letter highlighting the applicant’s qualifications and relevance to this search, a current curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests and plans (2 pages), a statement of teaching interests (2 pages), and contact information for three to five references. Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 01/15/2027 Open Until Filled No Requisition Number PRN01594CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Optional Documents University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210433": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Robotics in Advanced Manufacturing",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant/Associate/Professor of Robotics in Advanced Manufacturing Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate/Professor of Robotics in Advanced Manufacturing Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant/Associate/Professor Department 00068 - Mechanical Engineering City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 07/01/2027 Details The Department of Mechanical Engineering at the University of Utah ( https://mech.utah.edu/ ) invites applications for a tenured or tenure-track faculty position, with a Fall Semester 2027 starting date. Candidates with exceptional background and experience in areas of robotics in advanced manufacturing are strongly encouraged to apply. Candidates will also be expected to be affiliated with the University of Utah Robotics Center ( https://robotics.coe.utah.edu ), which manages the Robotics Ph.D. and M.S. degree programs as well as Robotics certificates and minors across three different departments in the College of Engineering. All areas of manufacturing that utilize advanced robotic concepts will be considered. The ideal candidate would be able to perform scholarly research with graduate students earning degrees in Mechanical Engineering and/or Robotics, and would also be able to collaborate with local industry on projects of shared interest. Exceptional candidates will be considered at the level of associate or full professor, with senior applicants with strong leadership in robotics research and education especially encouraged to apply. Candidates must have a demonstrated track record of high-quality research as evidenced by scholarly publications, and they must exhibit strong potential (junior faculty) or an established record (senior faculty) of securing extramural funding with highly regarded scholarly publications. Successful candidates will be able to actively contribute to the Department’s growing research presence in their respective areas. Additionally, candidates are expected to teach core courses and/or develop new courses that support the Mechanical Engineering and/or Robotics graduate programs. Applicants are expected to have an earned Ph.D. or Sc.D. in Mechanical Engineering, Robotics, or a closely related field. The University of Utah is a research-focused institution that is home to more than 35,000 students, world-class faculty and researchers, a medical campus, and many start-up companies. The Department of Mechanical Engineering has experienced tremendous growth over the past decade, fueled by the State of Utah’s Engineering Initiative, and currently houses more than 45 tenure-line faculty members, over 1,500 undergraduate and 250 graduate students (140 PhD students). The University of Utah campus is situated in Salt Lake City, a growing, increasingly diverse, metropolitan city with a population of 1M nestled against the backdrop of the beautiful Wasatch Mountains. The greater SLC valley is an important economic hub of the mountain west and is home to a number of large technology and fortune 500 companies. SLC residents enjoy a highly accessible and walkable downtown with vibrant restaurants, sports, nightlife, and cultural events. Salt Lake City residents enjoy easy access to national parks (8 within a few hours’ drive), world-class skiing/snowboarding (7 resorts within 1 hour), hiking, fishing, biking, and rafting/kayaking. In addition, faculty members enjoy free access to public transportation and the convenience of an international airport located only 15 minutes from campus. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates All applications received by January 15, 2027 will receive full consideration. Applications must be submitted electronically and should include: a cover letter highlighting the applicant’s qualifications and relevance to this search, current curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests and plans (2 pages), a statement of teaching interests (2 pages), and contact information for three to five references. Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 01/15/2027 Open Until Filled No Requisition Number PRN01593CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Optional Documents University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210345": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Cancer Biology, Department of Oncological Sciences",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Faculty Position in Cancer Biology, Department of Oncological Sciences Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Faculty Position in Cancer Biology, Department of Oncological Sciences Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Position/Rank Faculty Position in Cancer Biology, Department of Oncological Sciences Department 00222 - Oncological Sciences City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin Details The Department of Oncological Sciences and Huntsman Cancer Institute ( HCI ) invite applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the assistant professor level. We seek an outstanding cancer researcher who will complement and extend existing strengths in the Department. HCI provides an outstanding environment, with state-of-the-art facilities, excellent shared resources, access to clinical samples for research, and NCI Comprehensive Cancer Center designation in a collegial and collaborative Department culture. We seek an outstanding scientist centered on cancer biology and/or data science programs that address fundamental questions in cancer. Candidates should lead innovative cancer-focused programs in areas such as hematologic and solid tumors, including children’s and hormone-driven cancers or melanoma, tumor cell microenvironment, metastasis, signaling, metabolism, structural biology, or therapeutics. Candidates’ research programs should apply modern and innovative cancer research approaches, such as genetically engineered and patient-derived cancer models, genomics, data science, and/or artificial intelligence approaches to the above-mentioned areas. Applicants for Assistant Professor are expected to hold a PhD or MD/PhD (or equivalent), to have excelled in their postdoctoral training, and to have a track record of impact and research productivity. Candidates should submit a curriculum vitae, a cover letter containing a description of professional experience (including scientific accomplishments, leadership responsibilities, and 3 references), and a 3-page research plan. We encourage applications from candidates of all backgrounds. Candidates are encouraged to apply by November 30, 2026, at the following link: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209560. Huntsman Cancer Institute Attn: Recruitment Office, 2000 Circle of Hope, Salt Lake City, UT 84112-5550 Email: hci.recruitment@hci.utah.edu . About the University of Utah Health The University of Utah Health (U of U Health) is a patient-focused center distinguished by collaboration, excellence, leadership, and respect. The U of U Health values candidates who are committed to fostering and furthering the culture of compassion, collaboration, innovation, accountability, acceptance, integrity, quality, and trust that is integral to our mission. About Huntsman Cancer Institute As part of the University of Utah, the current Huntsman Cancer Institute enterprise includes over 300 cancer-focused research teams and has established outstanding programs in laboratory research, clinical cancer research, and cancer control and population sciences, and cross-disciplinary disease centers. HCI is the only NCI -designated Comprehensive Cancer Center in the Mountain West, and provides care for cancer patients in Utah, Idaho, Montana, Nevada, and Wyoming. With over 1 million square feet dedicated to research and clinical care, it provides patient and prevention education to residents from across the Mountain West and beyond. The institute uniquely and seamlessly integrates the cancer hospital with research buildings, enhancing the connection between cutting-edge research and clinical care. HCI has an outstanding history of academic achievement and impact and a commitment to excellence in patient care, research, teaching, and service. Our integrated health system and HCI’s participation in the ORIEN network of leading cancer centers provide opportunities for multidisciplinary collaborations across basic and translational laboratory-based cancer research, epidemiology and biostatistics, as well as access to extensive biospecimen repositories. HCI and the University of Utah are known for their highly collaborative and collegial culture, where faculty work across disciplines to solve the most pressing challenges in cancer care. In addition, the University of Utah and HCI are home to superb shared resources, such as the Utah Population Database with more than 16 million records linking genealogies, health records, and vital statistics, as well as the Utah Cancer Registry , which is a long-standing and preeminent SEER site. More information is available online for U of U Health Cores and Cancer Center Shared Resources . EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service, may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 10/05/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN04134F Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). One measure of faculty diversity at the School of Medicine is the proportion of faculty members who come from a rural background. Did you graduate high school from a town with less than 10,000 persons? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement List of References Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210263": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Position/Rank Assistant Professor Department 00196 - Health & Kinesiology City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin July 1, 2027 Details University of Utah, College of Health Department of Health & Kinesiology Assistant Professor – Tenure Track The University of Utah’s Department of Health & Kinesiology is seeking to hire two faculty members at the rank of Assistant Professor on the Tenure Track who can develop and lead research programs that aim to improve, restore and/or optimize Human Performance , broadly defined. Human Performance includes maximizing human capacity in healthy individuals, special populations – including individuals with chronic disease, disability, or injury – and individuals across the lifespan. Candidates with expertise and experience in one or one or more of the following areas are encouraged to apply: · Exercise physiology · Sports science · Rehabilitation · Wearable technologies · Neuromechanics · Neuromodulation · Neural control of movement The ability to build and/or strengthen collaborations within the Department, across the College of Health as well as the University of Utah Health Sciences and Academic campuses is desirable. APPOINTMENT · Rank: Assistant Professor · Track: Tenure Track · Number of positions: Two Primary Responsibilities Primary responsibilities include: · Establish and maintain an innovative, collaborative, and externally funded program of research. · Disseminate research findings through high-quality peer-reviewed publications and presentations at national and international scientific meetings. · Contribute to collaborative and interdisciplinary research within the Department, College of Health, University of Utah Health, and broader University community. · Teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses in areas of expertise within the Health & Kinesiology program, specifically related to human performance (broadly defined). · Mentor undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral students and contribute to the Department’s research-intensive MS and PhD programs; and applied MS program. · Contribute to the intellectual and scholarly activities in Human Performance (broadly defined). · Participate in appropriate service to the Department, College, University, profession, and/or community. Minimum qualifications Applicants must have: · An earned doctorate (PhD, DrPH, ScD, or equivalent) in kinesiology, or another discipline relevant to the areas mentioned above. · Evidence of the potential to develop an independent and impactful program of research. · A record of peer-reviewed scholarship appropriate to career stage. · Evidence of, or strong potential for, obtaining competitive extramural research funding; · Evidence of, or potential for, excellence in teaching and mentoring students. · Strong potential to contribute to collaborative and interdisciplinary research. Preferred qualifications · Postdoctoral research experience. Application Instructions Applicants must apply online at: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209711 Applicants should submit the following materials: 1. Cover letter (1-page limit) describing the applicant’s interest in the position and alignment with the Department of Health & Kinesiology. 2. Curriculum vitae . 3. Research statement (3-page limit) describing the applicant’s prior scholarship, current research program, and plans for developing an independent and externally funded program of research. 4. Teaching and mentoring statement (2-page limit) describing the applicant’s teaching philosophy, relevant experience, and approach to mentoring undergraduate and graduate students; and 5. List of three professional references, including names and contact information Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later stage of the search. Review of applications will begin on November 1 and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. About the Department and University The University of Utah is a Carnegie Research I institution located in Salt Lake City, in the foothills of the majestic Wasatch Range of the Rocky Mountains. The University of Utah academic and health sciences campuses are adjacently located, facilitating seamless trans- and interdisciplinary research collaborations. The Department of Health and Kinesiology is part of the College of Health and University of Utah Health Sciences. The Department of Health & Kinesiology’s undergraduate and graduate program is on the leading edge of training future scholars, health professionals and leaders in health sciences, with approximately 1,700 students registered in bachelors, masters and PhD degree programs. These programs are accredited by the National Academy of Kinesiology and Commission on Accreditation of Allied Health Education Programs, with the graduate program consistently ranking as one of the top programs in the country. Faculty in the Department of Health and Kinesiology have access to the College of Health’s state-of-the-science research facility, a resource-rich environment managed by a full-time lab manager and containing seven research laboratories: · Exercise Intervention Laboratory · Exercise Testing Laboratory · Wet Laboratory · Sleep, Circadian Physiology and Cognition Suite · Metabolic Kitchen · Mobility and Stability Laboratory · Musculoskeletal Biomechanics Laboratory Faculty also have access to 24 core research facilities available at the University ( cores.utah.edu ) and the opportunity to collaborate with other entities across the academic and health sciences campuses including the Clinical and Translational Sciences Institute, Center for Metabolic Health, Center on Aging, Huntsman Mental Health Institute, Huntsman Cancer Institute, the Responsible Artificial Intelligence Initiative, Digital Health Initiative, College of Health Community Outreach and Engagement, among others. Faculty in the Department of Health and Kinesiology have afforded success in obtaining extramural funding such as support from NIH , CDC , DOD and NSF . The University of Utah’s distinguished partnership with the International Olympic Committee ( IOC )—one of only eleven such partnerships worldwide—underscores its international leadership in applied health research, human performance, and athlete health and well-being. With the 2034 Olympic and Paralympic Winter Games returning to Salt Lake City, the Health & Kinesiology Program is uniquely positioned to advance and showcase innovative research, education, human performance, and community health and wellness initiatives associated with the Games. This environment provides exceptional opportunities for faculty to develop interdisciplinary collaborations and contribute to research and programs with local, national, and global impact. Living in Salt Lake City Life Salt Lake City, Utah and the surrounding area is exceptional. With easy access to eight exploring in nearby canyons and in the spectacular red-rock country of southern Utah. Ten National Parks are within a day driving distance of the University. Salt Lake City also offers professional symphony, ballet, modern dance, opera and theater performances, art galleries, world-class restaurants, and professional sports. Inquires Inquires may be sent to: Dr. Tim Brusseau Professor and Chair Department of Health & Kinesiology College of Health Tel: 801.587.7900 tim.brusseau@utah.edu OR Dr. Bradley King, Co-Chair Assistant Professor Department of Health & Kinesiology College of Health Bradley.Ross.King@utah.edu OR Dr. Adriana Coletta, Co-Chair Associate Professor Department of Health & Kinesiology College of Health Adriana.Coletta@hci.utah.edu University of Utah Health University of Utah Health (U of U Health) is a patient focused center distinguished by collaboration, excellence, leadership, and respect. U of U Health values candidates who are committed to fostering and furthering the culture of compassion, collaboration, innovation, accountability, acceptance, integrity, quality, and trust that is integral to our mission. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service, may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Applicants must apply online at: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209711 Applicants should submit the following materials: 1. Cover letter (1-page limit) describing the applicant’s interest in the position and alignment with the Department of Health & Kinesiology. 2. Curriculum vitae . 3. Research statement (3-page limit) describing the applicant’s prior scholarship, current research program, and plans for developing an independent and externally funded program of research. 4. Teaching and mentoring statement (2-page limit) describing the applicant’s teaching philosophy, relevant experience, and approach to mentoring undergraduate and graduate students; and 5. List of three professional references, including names and contact information Letters of recommendation may be requested at a later stage of the search. Review of applications will begin on November 1 and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027. Open Date 09/28/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN04138F Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement List of References Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210248": {
      "title": "Dean of the College of Fine Arts",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Dean of the College of Fine Arts Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Dean of the College of Fine Arts Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | This posting does not accept online applications. Please contact your Human Resource Office for instructions on how to apply. Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Dean Department 00002 - EVPAA - Academics City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 7/1/2027 Details The University of Utah seeks a Dean of the College of Fine Arts Click here to view position profile: https://summitsearchsolutions.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/UU_Dean_FineArts_Profile.pdf Location: Salt Lake City, Utah The Position: Reporting to the Executive Vice President for Academic Affairs and serving on the Council of Academic Deans, the Dean is the chief academic and administrative officer of the College of Fine Arts. The Dean provides strategic leadership across the College’s academic programs, research and creative activities, performance and exhibition spaces, and community of faculty, staff, and students. The Dean will articulate a compelling and sustainable vision for the College; support excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, and creative activity; oversee financial, human, and physical resources; recruit and retain outstanding faculty and staff; and serve as the College’s chief advocate and fundraiser. The next Dean will join the College at a time of opportunity and challenge. Key priorities include addressing aging and capacity-constrained facilities; strengthening support for faculty research and creative activity; expanding philanthropic support; and fostering interdisciplinary collaboration, including emerging opportunities at the intersection of arts and health. The Dean will be a visible advocate for the arts within the University and throughout Salt Lake City and Utah, strengthening the College’s relationships, reputation, reach, and impact. Qualifications: The successful candidate will possess: A terminal degree or equivalent professional accomplishment in an arts-related discipline, with a record appropriate for appointment as a tenured professor in the College. Significant academic leadership and administrative experience, including strategic planning, budget and resource management, personnel management, and organizational change. A strong understanding of the distinctive academic, creative, research, performance, production, and facilities needs of arts disciplines. A demonstrated commitment to excellence in teaching, research, scholarship, and creative activity. A record of effectively engaging faculty, staff, students, academic leaders, donors, community partners, and other stakeholders. A record of fostering a welcoming educational and professional environment for students, faculty, and staff from a range of backgrounds and experiences. The successful candidate will also demonstrate many of the following: A compelling vision for arts education, research, and creative practice within a public research university. A collaborative, transparent leadership style and ability to build trust and shared priorities. Exceptional communication, interpersonal, listening, and problem-solving skills. Success advocating for resources and addressing complex facilities and space needs. Experience advancing research, scholarship, and creative activity. Success in fundraising, development, community engagement, and external relations. An ability to articulate the value of the arts to diverse audiences. An interest in interdisciplinary collaboration and new opportunities for the arts. An engaged leadership presence and enthusiasm for participating in the artistic and creative life of the College. To Apply: The responsibilities, qualifications, and preferences listed in this posting are intended to give applicants an understanding of the role we are seeking to fill and the types of skills, experience, and education that may have prepared someone to be effective in this role. Please submit application materials that demonstrate how your experience, leadership record, and professional accomplishments align with the qualifications and priorities of the position and describe your interest in serving as Dean of the College of Fine Arts at the University of Utah. For more information and to apply to this position please apply here: https://theapplicantmanager.com/jobs?pos=su528 Confidential review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled; individuals who apply by November 6, 2026 will be given first consideration . Upon application, please submit a PDF version of your CV/resume and a cover letter. All applications and nominations will be held in confidence. Refer to our blog on strengthening application materials and letters of interest here . EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01599CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/210145": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor in The Department of Management",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Associate Professor or Professor in The Department of Management Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Associate Professor or Professor in The Department of Management Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Tenured Department 00043 - Management Department City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 07/01/2027 Details The Department of Management at the University of Utah’s David Eccles School of Business (https://eccles.utah.edu) seeks to hire a senior scholar in Management/Organizational Behavior – either a Full Professor or a senior Associate Professor – to fill a tenured position at the rank of Full Professor or Associate Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. Ideal qualifications for this position include: PhD in Management or Organizational Behavior, or in closely related field, with an established scholarly and professional identity in Management/Organizational Behavior; Established and sustained record of high-quality scholarship published predominantly in top-tier Management journals; A recent record of research productivity and a strong, active pipeline indicating the likelihood of continued publication in top-tier Management journals; Research program that enhances and expands the intellectual environment of the Department and School; Demonstrated excellence in teaching, including EMBA and MBA courses; History of effective leadership and service at the department, college, and/or university levels; Experience building positive, collaborative relationships within and across departments; Commitment to professional and constructive interactions with colleagues. The tenure-track faculty in the Management Department includes Bailey Bigelow, Bryan Bonner, Kristina Diekmann, Jesse Graham, Glen Kreiner, Kylie Rochford, Amelia Stillwell, David Tannenbaum, and Elizabeth Tenney. With a shared interest in the dynamics of organizational life, this group of scholars conducts research using a broad range of research methods on topics including, but not limited to, ethical decision making in organizations, individual and group decision making processes, intergroup conflict and inequality, negotiations, status and hierarchy, stigma, workplace relationships, mental health, moral judgment, organizational justice, workplace mistreatment, and identity processes. This work is published in various top-tier outlets. Our department values open science as appropriately applied to a wide variety of research methods (quantitative, qualitative, mixed-method). The University of Utah is located in Salt Lake City, the capital of Utah which is the fastest-growing tech state in the U.S. and home to the “Silicon Slopes” agglomeration of technology companies, including significant operations for Adobe, eBay, Intel, Micron, and DOMO . The University is within a day’s drive of five national parks. Nine ski and snowboard resorts are nestled in the mountains above Salt Lake City, which hosted the Winter Olympic Games in 2002 and has been selected to host the Olympic Games again in 2034. The American Economic Development Institute named Utah the number one pro-business state in the country, the first state west of the Mississippi River to earn this recognition, and noted the state’s leadership in economic development, innovation, and entrepreneurship. The University of Utah is a member of the Association of American Universities, a prestigious, invitation-only group of 71 leading research institutions marked by excellence in academic expertise and research impact, student success, and the ability to secure resources in support of their core missions. The University is on the rise, as measured by nearly every key indicator, from graduation rates to research funding, to support from private and public partners. The Eccles School of Business has made similar significant gains. As the University aspires to become a top-10 public university, senior faculty in the Eccles School will play a key role in shaping the future contributions of the Business School in support of this mission. Review will begin October 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 09/11/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01585CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement Teaching Evaluations Example of Research 1 Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only Example of Research 2 Example of Research 3 University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
