    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209909": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Environmental Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant/Associate Professor in Environmental Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate Professor in Environmental Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant/Associate Professor Department 00058 - Civil and Environmental Engin City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 08/01/2027 Details The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering ( CVEEN ) at the University of Utah invites applications for two 9-month, tenure-track faculty position in Environmental Engineering with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. The positions are anticipated to be filled at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. Position Description The successful applicant(s) will be required to develop an externally funded research program, teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Civil and Environmental, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, produce scholarly publications, collaborate with other faculty on professional development interests, and provide departmental, college and university service. An earned Ph.D. degree in Civil Engineering, Environmental Engineering, or a closely-related discipline before the date of hire is required. Strong communication skills and the ability to work collegially and collaboratively with diverse internal and external constituencies are also necessary. Professional engineering licensure is highly desired or the candidate should have the potential to get a P.E. soon after their appointment. The successful candidate(s) will have a skillset that complements and expands existing expertise in the department. We invite candidates with a demonstrated record of innovative, high-impact research across the broad field of environmental engineering. Areas of particular interest include, but are not limited to: environmental and analytical chemistry, geochemistry, and/or reactive transport in surface, subsurface and engineered systems; environmental biotechnology and bioprocess engineering; emerging contaminants and their detection, fate, transport, treatment, and remediation; advanced water and wastewater treatment, including membrane and nanomaterial-based technologies; resource recovery, waste valorization, and circular-economy approaches, including recovery of critical materials; environmental sensing and real-time monitoring; and data-driven environmental engineering, including artificial intelligence, machine learning, digital twins, and intelligent environmental systems. Candidates whose research creates new connections among environmental engineering, water, energy, infrastructure, natural systems, and emerging technologies are also encouraged to apply. Candidates with strong core fundamentals that allow them to work across disciplinary boundaries are particularly encouraged to apply. We are seeking highly motivated team players looking to help advance our exciting undergraduate and graduate engineering programs and contribute to the Department’s online education efforts. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the newly added environmental emphasis for undergraduate students. Assistant professors are supported by a reduced teaching load of one course per semester until tenure to aid in the development of a robust research portfolio during the pre-tenure period. Part of the Big 12 Conference, the University of Utah is a Carnegie Research 1 University located in Salt Lake City at the foothills of the beautiful Wasatch Mountains. Founded in 1850, the University of Utah now enrolls over 38,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The area experiences the four seasons and is known for world-class outdoor recreational activities including skiing, hiking, and biking. The State of Utah has more national parks and scenic areas than any other state. Growth in transportation, energy, mining, software, semiconductors, finance, education, healthcare, and business job sectors have contributed to the State’s prosperity, despite the difficult national economy. The University and college support creative ideas through competitive seed funding via the college seed and instrumentation grants, 1U4U program and others. The Civil and Environmental Engineering department is one of seven rapidly growing departments within the John and Marcia Price College of Engineering. In the past decade, the number of tenure-track faculty positions has grown by 50% and research expenditures have more than tripled, to $61 million per year, placing our College in the top 30 engineering universities in the country for research volume (2022). The CVEEN Department currently has 22 tenure track faculty members, five lecturing faculty, plus we are actively searching for five positions including these vacancies. Our enrollment is 100 full-time graduate students, and 440 undergraduate students. The department has an active research program with over $19 million in external funding in 2023. Additional information about the Department is available at: www.civil.utah.edu. Application Process – Electronic application materials (pdf format) should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research interests, statement of teaching interests, up to three (3) relevant publications, and contact information for three references. Initial screening of applicants will begin November 15, 2026 although applications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is closed. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 09/21/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01588CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this position? Please specify the name of the job board, community, education, government agency, search engine, etc. used to find out about this position. (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Example of Research 1 Optional Documents Example of Research 2 Example of Research 3 University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209901": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Structural Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor in Structural Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor in Structural Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant Professor Department 00058 - Civil and Environmental Engin City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 08/01/2027 Details The Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering ( CVEEN ) at the University of Utah invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position in the area of Structural Engineering with an anticipated start date of August 1st, 2027. This position is anticipated to be filled at the Assistant Professor level. Position Description – The successful applicant will be required to develop an externally funded research program, teach undergraduate and graduate courses in structural engineering, mentor undergraduate and graduate students, produce scholarly publications, collaborate with other faculty on professional development interests, and provide departmental, college, university, and national professional service. An earned Ph.D. degree in Civil Engineering or a closely related discipline before the date of hire is required. Strong communication skills and the ability to work collegially and collaboratively with diverse internal and external constituencies are also necessary. The candidate shall hold a PE license or be on the path of obtaining a PE license and be able to teach structural design courses. To address the transformative leaps in structural engineering technologies, innovative research and teaching approaches will require inspirational leaders with adaptive abilities in areas including (but not limited to): • Trustworthy AI-Enabled Structural Engineering and Digital Twins: Development of physics-informed, domain-guided, and uncertainty-aware artificial intelligence for structural analysis, design, optimization, and risk assessment. Interests include sensor-to-model translation, computer vision, scientific machine learning, surrogate and reduced-order models, inverse design, and interoperable digital twins that update asset condition and support transparent engineering decisions rather than black-box prediction. • Infrastructure Integrity, Structural Health Monitoring, and Lifecycle Extension: Sensing, noncontact imaging, remote sensing, UAVs/robotics, and data-model fusion for detecting damage and deterioration in buildings, bridges, and energy infrastructure. Emphasis includes fatigue, fracture, corrosion, durability, remaining-capacity and service-life prediction, risk-based inspection and maintenance, targeted repair, and self-repairing or bio-inspired approaches that extend the useful life of existing infrastructure. • Multi-Hazard and Climate-Resilient Structural Systems: Performance-based assessment, design, retrofit, and recovery under earthquakes, hurricane and tornado winds, flooding, wildfire and wildland-urban-interface fire, post-fire slope hazards, landslides, and other compound or cascading events. Interests include probabilistic and uncertainty-informed methods, nonstationary climate and exposure, infrastructure interdependencies, and links from component response to network function, and resilience of rural and energy systems. • Advanced Computational Mechanics: Multiscale and multiphysics methods for failure, fracture, nonlinear dynamics, fluid-structure interaction, geohazard-structure interaction, and coupled soil-structure systems. Relevant approaches include high-performance computing, open and reproducible simulation frameworks, hybrid physics-data models, and thermo-hydro-mechanical formulations for underground, coastal, nuclear, geothermal, carbon-storage, and other infrastructure operating under severe or evolving conditions. • Low-Carbon, Architected, and Digitally Manufactured Structural Materials: Design, processing, mechanics, fabrication and qualification of high-performance and sustainable construction materials, including low-carbon and carbon-storing cementitious systems, UHPC , mass timber, fiber-reinforced composites, multi-functional materials, metamaterials, metastructures, bio-inspired and bio-mimicking structures and materials to optimize system performance and sustainability in the built environment. Interests include additive and automated construction, digital fabrication, topology-informed reinforcement, cold-spray and other repair technologies, and structure-property-durability relationships across scales. • Experimental, Field, and Cyber-Physical Validation: Large-scale laboratory and field testing, wind and fire experiments, shake-table studies, integration of cyber-physical systems (e.g., NSF NHERI ), development of hybrid simulation techniques that enhance understanding of structural behaviors under extreme or coupled excitations, including corrosion and time-dependent degradation, and advanced material characterization that connect mechanics, sensing, and computation. Priority is placed on benchmark datasets and validation methods that quantify uncertainty, test transferability, support human-AI teaming, and translate research into inspection practice, performance-based design, retrofit guidance, codes, standards, and deployable decision tools. Candidates with strong core fundamentals that allow them to work across disciplinary boundaries are particularly encouraged to apply. We are seeking highly motivated team players who are looking to help advance our exciting undergraduate and graduate engineering programs. Being part of the Big 12 Conference, the University of Utah is a Carnegie Research 1 University located in Salt Lake City at the foothills of the beautiful Wasatch Mountains. Founded in 1850, the University of Utah now enrolls over 36,000 undergraduate and graduate students. The area experiences the four seasons and is known for world-class outdoor recreational activities including skiing, hiking, and biking. The State of Utah has more national parks and scenic areas than any other state. Growth in transportation, energy, mining, software, semiconductors, finance, education, healthcare, and business job sectors have contributed to the State’s prosperity, despite the difficult national economy. The CVEEN department is one of seven rapidly growing departments within the John and Marcia Price College of Engineering. In the past decade, the number of tenure-track faculty positions has grown by 50% and research expenditures have more than tripled, to $81.5 million per year, placing our College in the top 30 engineering universities in the country for research volume (2022). The CVEEN department currently has 22 tenure track faculty members and 5 lecturing faculty, plus we are actively searching for 5 positions including this vacancy. Our enrollment is 100 full-time graduate students, and 440 undergraduate students. The department has an active research program with over $19 million in external funding in 2023. Additional information about the department is available at: www.civil.utah.edu . Application Process – Electronic application materials (pdf format) should include a cover letter, curriculum vitae, statement of research interests, statement of teaching interests, up to three (3) relevant publications, and contact information for three references. Initial screening of applicants will begin November 1, 2026 although applications will be accepted and reviewed until the position is closed. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 09/08/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01578CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this position? Please specify the name of the job board, community, education, government agency, search engine, etc. used to find out about this position. (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Research Statement Teaching Statement Example of Research 1 Optional Documents Example of Research 2 Example of Research 3 Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209900": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Nuclear Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor in Nuclear Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor in Nuclear Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant Professor Department 00058 - Civil and Environmental Engin City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 08/01/2027 Details The University of Utah’s Nuclear Engineering Program ( UNEP ), within the John and Marcia Price College of Engineering, invites applications for up to two full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in Nuclear Engineering, starting August 1, 2027. UNEP is part of a rapidly growing Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering ( CVEEN ), which has expanded its tenure-track faculty by 50% in the past decade and now manages over $8.3 million in annual research expenditures. The department includes 22 tenure-track faculty, five lecturers, and benefits from more than $16 million in external research funding (2025). Detailed information is at www.civil.utah.edu . The program features four core tenure-track faculty and draws on expertise from affiliated faculty across Engineering, Science, and Medicine. UNEP offers an undergraduate minor and M.S. and Ph.D. degrees, and operates an NRC -licensed 100 kW TRIGA reactor with state-of-the-art facilities. Research areas span nuclear energy, advanced reactor designs, fuel cycles, safety, nuclear medicine, advanced modeling, fusion, and AI/ML applications. Faculty have access to premier research resources, including the Nanofab materials characterization center. In response to growing demand for nuclear energy, UNEP seeks applicants in all areas of nuclear engineering, with particular interest in: (1) Thermal-hydraulics; (2) Nuclear fuel cycles for current and next-generation systems; (3) Nuclear fusion science and technology; (4) AI/ML relevant to nuclear engineering; and (5) Radiological Science. Position expectations include: (1) Teaching undergraduate and graduate nuclear engineering courses; (2) Developing an externally funded research program; (3) Contributing to curriculum development and program improvement; (4) Mentoring undergraduate and graduate students; and (5) Participating in service, outreach, and recruitment activities. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in nuclear engineering or a closely related field by the date of hire, strong communication skills, and a commitment to teaching and research. The successful candidate is also expected to develop a strong externally funded research program and to be eligible to serve as a principal investigator on relevant U.S. federal nuclear research funding opportunities, including U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission ( NRC ) grant programs, Department of Homeland Security, NNSA and others in accordance with applicable sponsor eligibility requirements. The University of Utah is an R1 research institution located in Salt Lake City, at the base of the Wasatch Mountains. Utah was ranked the Best State to Live In (US News, 2026), and the University is the #1 Public University in the West (Wall Street Journal, 2024). To Apply: Submit a cover letter, CV, statements of research and teaching interests, up to three relevant publications(all as PDF format), and contact information for three references. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Open Date 09/08/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01575CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this position? Please specify the name of the job board, community, education, government agency, search engine, etc. used to find out about this position. (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement Example of Research 1 Optional Documents Example of Research 2 Example of Research 3 University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209889": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the Department of City &amp; Metropolitan Planning",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor in the Department of City &amp; Metropolitan Planning Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor in the Department of City & Metropolitan Planning Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant Professor Department 00659 - City & Metropolitan Planning City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 7/1/27 Details Tenure Track Assistant Professor in Housing and/or Economic Development The Department of City & Metropolitan Planning at the University of Utah seeks to fill a tenure-track position at the Assistant Professor level to begin in Fall 2027. We invite applicants with interest in housing and/or economic development, including planning for social justice or in underserved communities. Of particular interest to the Department are candidates with expertise in mixed methods, quantitative, and/or geospatial approaches. Candidates with expertise in other methodological approaches are also welcome to apply. Candidates may have research interests in the U.S. or internationally and should demonstrate an interest in developing a research program focused on Utah and the Intermountain West, including the region’s small towns and rural communities. A doctoral degree in planning or a related field is required by July 1, 2027. Qualifications: Applicants should hold a doctoral degree in urban and regional planning or a related field by July 1, 2027, and hold interest in housing and/or economic development. Commensurate with their level of experience, candidates must demonstrate an excellent record of research publications, a strong potential for securing competitive external funding, and a commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring students. Expectations: Successful candidates are expected to develop and maintain an ongoing program of scholarly research. They should demonstrate the ability to produce high-quality scholarship while fostering interdisciplinary collaborations. Successful candidates are expected to teach in-person courses and provide mentorship across the BA/BS in Urban Ecology, Master’s in City & Metropolitan Planning and PhD in Metropolitan Planning, Policy and Design curriculum to students on the Salt Lake City Campus. Candidates will also be expected to contribute to department, college, university and professional service. Review of applications will begin on November 1, 2026. Inquiries regarding the position may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. C. Aujean Lee ( aujean.lee@utah.edu ), with the subject header, “CMP Assistant Professor Posting.” Qualified applicants must submit: · A maximum 2-page cover letter explaining interest in the position; · Curriculum vitae; · A maximum 2-page research statement addressing current and future interests; · A maximum 2-page teaching statement including philosophy, experience, interests, and approach to student mentorship; · A list of three references. References will not be contacted prior to receiving the candidate’s consent to contact. Candidates may be asked to provide additional information during the search process. About the Department of City & Metropolitan Planning: The successful candidate will join a dynamic group of engaged faculty members who are recognized leaders in their fields and highly value collaboration and collegiality. This person will also benefit from the University of Utah’s strong commitment to sustainability and interdisciplinary initiatives. The Department’s BA/BS undergraduate degree in Urban Ecology focuses on exploring the interrelationships among social, environmental, and economic systems, aiming to enhance the vitality and sustainability of places and communities. The Urban Ecology degree is also taught at the Utah Asia Campus in Songdo, South Korea. The Master of City and Metropolitan Planning ( MCMP ) is an accredited professional degree emphasizing ecological resiliency, smart growth, and social justice through effective communication, collaboration, and innovation. The Ph.D. in Metropolitan Planning, Policy, and Design is a research-focused program that trains academic leaders of the future. The department also offers a Master of Real Estate Development (with School of Business) and Graduate Certificates in Urban Design, Community Engaged Practices, and Real Estate Development. Applicants are encouraged to consult the department website at https://plan.cap.utah.edu/. The Department of City & Metropolitan Planning is committed to building a welcoming intellectual community and encourages applicants of all racial/ethnic groups, genders, backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives to apply. About the College of Architecture and Planning: The University of Utah College of Architecture and Planning (CA+P) is a community of award-winning faculty, entrepreneurial students, and support staff that together form a creative community unlike any other. The College is home to four academic units: the School of Architecture, the Department of City and Metropolitan Planning, the Division of Games, and the Division of Multi-Disciplinary Design, as well as multiple research centers and collaboratives. Across these units are top-ranked academic programs, outstanding researchers with a strong commitment to addressing defining issues of our time, and a record of unsurpassed societal impact. As part of a campus that values innovation and interdisciplinarity, the College is transforming its disciplines and training graduates to become leaders and changemakers in the built and virtual environments globally. More information is available at cap.utah.edu. About the University: The University of Utah (the U) is the state’s flagship institution of higher education, with 18 schools and colleges, more than 100 undergraduate and 90 graduate degree programs, and an enrollment of more than 38,500 students. In 2019, the University joined the Association of American Universities, an invitation-only, prestigious group of 65 leading research institutions marked by excellence in academic expertise and research impact, student success, and securing resources in support of core missions, which are focused on serving the increasingly diverse student population at the U. As of Fall 2024, 25% of students identified as first generation and 28% of domestic incoming freshmen were students of color. The U is classified as having “highest research activity” (R-1 classification) by the Carnegie Foundation and is a member of the University Innovation Alliance, a coalition of 17 top public research universities dedicated to student success and innovation. In 2023, the University was ranked as the #1 public university in the West and #8 among public universities in the nation by The Wall Street Journal. The U is located in Salt Lake City, one of the most dynamic and fastest growing metropolitan areas in the U.S. The city is uniquely situated to offer both unmatched outdoor and exciting urban experiences, with mountain vistas only a few steps away and vast red-rock desert landscapes within reach of a scenic afternoon drive. Additionally, the Utah Asia Campus was established in 2014 and is located in the Songdo International Business District in South Korea. Students attending the Utah Asia Campus find the same world-class U.S. academic experience offered at the campus in Salt Lake City. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Required Documents: A maximum 2-page cover letter explaining interest in the position; Curriculum vitae A maximum 2-page research statement addressing current and future interests; A maximum 2-page teaching statement including philosophy, experience, interests, and approach to student mentorship; A list of three references. References will not be contacted prior to receiving the candidate’s consent to contact. Candidates may be asked to provide additional information during the search process. Open Date 09/15/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01586CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement List of References Personal Statement Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209875": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor for Materials Science and Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant/Associate Professor for Materials Science and Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant/Associate Professor for Materials Science and Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant/Associate Professor Department 00067 - Material Science and Engineer City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 7/1/2027 Details The Department of Materials Science and Engineering at the University of Utah invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the associate professor level in critical minerals and materials, with an emphasis on manufacturing, scale-up, and integration of these materials into real-world applications. Candidates at the assistant professor level with strong records and promise are also welcome to apply. A Ph.D. in materials science and engineering or an allied field is required. Critical minerals and materials are a strategic priority for the University of Utah and the State of Utah. For example, we have recently established the Institute for Critical and Strategic Materials (criticalminerals.utah.edu). The Department has deep strengths in the extraction, chemistry, physics, and processing of critical materials, and this position extends that work downstream toward the components, devices, and systems these materials enable. We are interested in colleagues who make, scale, and integrate critical-material components, for example permanent magnets, battery electrodes, wide-bandgap semiconductors, high-temperature alloys and coatings, or photonic and quantum materials, and who connect that work to industry and to the broader energy, electronics, and defense supply chains. These are examples rather than requirements; we welcome applicants whose work advances critical materials applications in other ways. The successful candidate will: • Build and sustain an externally funded research group in critical materials manufacturing and application integration • Teach and develop undergraduate and graduate courses in materials science and engineering, including materials processing, manufacturing, and applications • Collaborate across the Department, the Price College of Engineering, and campus, and partner with industry, national laboratories, and government • Contribute to student mentoring, recruitment, outreach, and professional education EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Required Documents to Upload: Cover letter (1 to 2 pages) Curriculum vitae Research statement (up to 4 pages) describing past accomplishments and a 5-year plan, with specific attention to manufacturing, scale-up, and application integration of critical materials Teaching statement (up to 2 pages) Names and contact information for at least four references (letters requested by the committee for finalists) Open Date 09/17/2026 Close Date 01/15/2027 Open Until Filled No Requisition Number PRN01587CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
