    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209426": {
      "title": "Open Rank, Tenure Track Position",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Open Rank, Tenure Track Position Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Open Rank, Tenure Track Position Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Position/Rank Open Rank, Tenure Track Position Department 00199 - Communication Sci & Disorders City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin July 1, 2027 Details Open-Rank, Tenure-Line Faculty Position Assistant/Associate/Professor – Dependent upon Qualifications The Department of Communication Sciences & Disorders ( CSD ) in the College of Health at the University of Utah invites applications for a full-time tenure-line faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, or Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027, or as negotiated. We seek an innovative scholar whose research, teaching, and service activities will contribute to the department’s mission of advancing discovery, education, and clinical practice in communication sciences and disorders. The Department is among the nation’s longstanding academic programs in speech-language pathology and audiology and will celebrate its centennial in 2027. Since its founding, the Department has maintained a strong tradition of excellence in education, research, and clinical service, and continues to expand its national impact through innovative scholarship, interdisciplinary collaboration, and community engagement. About the University of Utah The University of Utah is the state’s flagship public research university and a member of the Association of American Universities ( AAU ). The University fosters student success, advances discovery and innovation, and engages local and global communities to improve health and quality of life. Through its integrated academic health system, faculty benefit from close collaboration with University of Utah Health, the Spencer Fox Eccles School of Medicine, and numerous interdisciplinary centers and institutes that support basic, translational, clinical, engineering, and population health research. Position Overview The Department seeks to recruit a scholar with expertise in one or more of the following areas: · Voice disorders · Airway-related disorders · Swallowing disorders (dysphagia) · Neuromotor speech disorders · Speech, voice, or swallowing biomarkers · Digital health technologies · Artificial intelligence and computational approaches to communication disorders · Related areas of communication sciences and disorders The Department is particularly interested in candidates whose scholarship complements current strengths in voice, airway, swallowing, neuromotor communication disorders, movement disorders, and translational rehabilitation science. Applicants employing clinical, translational, engineering, computational, behavioral, implementation science, or team-science approaches are especially encouraged to apply. We seek a candidate who will strengthen the Department’s interdisciplinary research mission through the development or expansion of a competitive, externally fundable research program and who can contribute to large-scale collaborative research initiatives involving faculty across the University of Utah research enterprise. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: · Establish, maintain, and grow an internationally recognized program of research. · Pursue and sustain extramural funding from federal agencies, foundations, industry, and/or other external sources. · Conduct innovative research that advances scientific knowledge and improves outcomes for individuals with communication, voice, airway, swallowing, and/or neuromotor speech disorders. · Collaborate with faculty across the Department, College, University, and University of Utah Health. · Teach and mentor students in undergraduate, master’s, doctoral, and postdoctoral training programs, consistent with expertise and departmental needs. · Mentor graduate students and trainees in research, scholarship, and professional development. · Participate in departmental, college, university, and professional service activities. · Contribute to an academic community that values collaboration, mentorship, innovation, and belonging. Research Environment The Department offers a vibrant and expanding research environment aligned with its strategic goal of advancing innovative basic, translational, and clinical research. Faculty benefit from active interdisciplinary collaborations, shared research infrastructure, and opportunities to participate in federally funded initiatives spanning health sciences, engineering, rehabilitation, neuroscience, and clinical care. The Department supports numerous externally funded research programs and is committed to continued growth in research productivity, doctoral training, and collaborative scholarship. Opportunities for collaboration exist across numerous units, including: · University of Utah Health · Spencer Fox Eccles School of Medicine · Craig H. Neilsen Rehabilitation Hospital · Primary Children’s Hospital · Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery · Department of Neurology · Department of Neurosurgery · Department of Biomedical Engineering · Department of Population Health Sciences · College of Health · Additional centers and institutes supporting translational and clinical research Department Overview The mission of the Department of Communication Sciences and Disorders is to develop competent and caring audiologists, speech-language pathologists, and speech-language-hearing scientists; advance basic and clinical research; deliver exceptional clinical services; and provide outstanding educational experiences for students. As a research-intensive department with national and global reach, it is committed to the highest standards of teaching, scholarship, clinical service, and scientific discovery. The Department offers: · B.S. in Speech and Hearing Science · M.S. in Speech-Language Pathology · Doctor of Audiology (AuD) · PhD in Communication Sciences and Disorders These programs provide extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary teaching, mentorship, and collaborative research training. The Department currently supports a robust community of undergraduate, master’s, clinical doctoral, and PhD students and maintains strong ties to clinical and community partners throughout the region. Required Qualifications Applicants must possess: · An earned PhD, or equivalent research doctorate in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Speech-Language Pathology, Speech and Hearing Science, Rehabilitation Science, Neuroscience, Biomedical Engineering, Public Health, or a closely related field. · Evidence of scholarly productivity and research excellence appropriate for rank. · Demonstrated potential for, or record of, obtaining external research funding. · Commitment to excellence in teaching, mentorship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. · Eligibility to work in the United States. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates with one or more of the following: · Established record of externally funded research. · Expertise in voice, airway, swallowing, neuromotor speech, or related disorders. · Demonstrated success in interdisciplinary and team-based research. · Research incorporating innovative methodologies such as biomechanics, digital health technologies, artificial intelligence, machine learning, neuroimaging, computational modeling, clinical outcomes research, implementation science, or precision medicine. · Eligibility for ASHA certification and/or speech-language pathology licensure where relevant. · Experience mentoring graduate students, postdoctoral fellows, or junior investigators. · Experience contributing to multi-site, programmatic, or center-level research initiatives. Rank and Compensation Rank and salary will be commensurate with qualifications, experience, and scholarly accomplishments. Competitive start-up support and research resources will be provided consistent with University policies and available resources. Equal Opportunity Employer The University of Utah is an equal opportunity employer. The University is committed to creating an academic environment in which members of the University community have access to opportunities and resources to achieve their goals and can thrive in an environment of respect, belonging, and academic excellence. Application Instructions Applicants must apply online at: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209305 Applicants should include the following materials: · Cover letter · Curriculum vitae · Research statement · Teaching and mentoring statement · Names and contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed beginning immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Inquires Inquires may be sent to: Sean Redmond Professor Department of Communication Sciences & Disorders College of Health sean.redmond@health.utah.edu University of Utah Health (U of U Health) is a patient focused center distinguished by collaboration, excellence, leadership, and respect. U of U Health values candidates who are committed to fostering and furthering the culture of compassion, collaboration, innovation, accountability, acceptance, integrity, quality, and trust that is integral to our mission. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service, may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Application Instructions Applicants must apply online at: https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/209305 Applicants should include the following materials: · Cover letter · Curriculum vitae · Research statement · Teaching and mentoring statement · Names and contact information for three professional references Applications will be reviewed beginning immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Inquires Inquires may be sent to: Sean Redmond Professor Department of Communication Sciences & Disorders College of Health sean.redmond@health.utah.edu Open Date 09/17/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN04125F Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement List of References Optional Documents Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Description for More Information – Appropriate discharge document (such as DD-2214) – Veteran Only Historical Only – Do Not Use – See Document Description for More Information – Addendum to the University of Utah – Veteran Only University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
