    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/208372": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Lecturer) of Practice for Mining Engineering",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor (Lecturer) of Practice for Mining Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor (Lecturer) of Practice for Mining Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Proposed Faculty Rank Assistant Professor (Lecturer) Department 00111 - Mining Engineering City Salt Lake City, UT Track Non Tenure Track New Position to Begin December 1, 2026 Details Career-Line Faculty Position in Mining Engineering Teaching-Focused Appointment Department of Mining Engineering University of Utah The Department of Mining Engineering at the University of Utah invites applications for a career-line faculty position focused on undergraduate teaching and student success. This is a 12-month, teaching-focused appointment with an anticipated salary range of $80,000–$90,000, commensurate with qualifications and experience, and subject to University approval. The anticipated start date is 12/1/2026. About the Department The University of Utah Department of Mining Engineering is a forward-looking program committed to preparing students for productive careers in responsible mineral development in Utah, the western United States, and around the world. The department’s mission is to provide world-class training in mining engineering by leveraging the latest technology and teaching excellence. The department values safe and progressive mineral development, strong support for the University and regional stakeholders, and a collegial and professional environment where faculty, staff, and students thrive. The department has an active and experienced mining engineering faculty with strengths across a broad range of areas, including tailings and waste management, remote sensing, health and safety, rock mechanics, mine ventilation, AI and systems engineering, systems integration, and heat management. The program is strongly connected to industry and regional stakeholders, and its strategic direction emphasizes undergraduate curriculum strength, ABET accreditation, teaching facilities, student experience, industry case studies, and preparation of technically strong, digitally fluent, systems-oriented graduates who are ready to contribute from day one. Position Summary The successful candidate will play a central role in supporting the undergraduate Mining Engineering program and the department’s ABET -accredited degree. The position is intended for an experienced teacher and mining professional who is comfortable teaching a wide range of undergraduate mining engineering courses and who can connect classroom learning with practical mining applications. The appointment will carry a heavy teaching responsibility. The successful candidate will teach core and elective undergraduate courses, contribute to curriculum development and continuous improvement, support ABET assessment and documentation, advise and mentor undergraduate students, and help strengthen the student experience through applied projects, field-oriented learning, industry case studies, and professional preparation. This position is especially well suited for a candidate who has meaningful teaching experience, substantial industry experience, or both. A Ph.D. is preferred, but it is not required if the candidate’s teaching record, professional mining experience, and technical background demonstrate the ability to deliver high-quality undergraduate mining engineering instruction. Primary Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: • Teach undergraduate mining engineering courses across the curriculum, with possible areas including but not limited to introduction to mining, mine design, mine planning, surface and underground mining methods, mine ventilation, mine safety, mine systems, geospatial and surveying applications, mine operations, equipment, engineering economics, capstone design, and technical electives. • Support the ABET -accredited undergraduate program through assessment, continuous improvement, course documentation, and engagement with student outcomes. • Develop and deliver high-quality course materials that integrate engineering fundamentals, practical mining applications, modern digital tools, industry case studies, and systems thinking. • Mentor and advise undergraduate students, including support for capstone projects, internships, professional development, student design competitions, and career preparation. • Work collaboratively with tenure-line and career-line faculty to maintain a coherent undergraduate curriculum and strengthen the student value proposition. • Engage with industry partners, alumni, and stakeholders to bring practical mining experience into the classroom. • Contribute to department service appropriate to a teaching-focused career-line appointment such as recruitment events, faculty meetings, etc. Required Qualifications Applicants must have: • An earned bachelor’s degree in Mining Engineering (or a closely aligned engineering field). • Demonstrated ability or strong potential to teach undergraduate mining engineering courses effectively. • Significant experience in mining engineering education, mining industry practice, or a combination of both. • Ability to teach across a broad range of mining engineering topics. • Commitment to undergraduate student learning, professional preparation, and continuous curriculum improvement. • Strong communication skills and the ability to work collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, alumni, and industry partners. Preferred Qualifications Preferred candidates will have most of the following: • Ph.D. in Mining Engineering or a closely related field. • Master’s degree in Mining Engineering with substantial teaching and/or industry experience. • Significant professional experience in mine operations, mine planning, mine design, mine safety, ventilation, geomechanics, tailings, systems engineering, automation, digital mine technologies, or related areas. • Prior university-level teaching experience, especially in an ABET -accredited engineering program. • Experience with undergraduate curriculum development, course assessment, ABET documentation, or continuous improvement processes. • Experience using or teaching modern mining software, digital tools, data analytics, mine planning tools, geospatial tools, or systems-integration approaches. • Ability to develop industry-connected projects, case studies, field experiences, laboratories, or capstone design experiences. • Professional licensure or eligibility for professional licensure. • Experience mentoring student design competition team EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Required Documents to Upload: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Open Date 07/06/2026 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN01550CF Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Optional Documents Research Statement Teaching Evaluations Example of Research 1 Example of Research 2 Example of Research 3 Writing Sample 1 University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/208294": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the Tenure Track",
      "description": "University of Utah Employment Site | Assistant Professor in the Tenure Track Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor in the Tenure Track Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details The University of Utah, an AA/EO employer, encourages applications from all qualified individuals, and provides reasonable accommodation to the known disabilities of applicants and employees. The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students, staff, faculty and patients from all backgrounds and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education, employment opportunities, and quality healthcare for historically underrepresented groups. Position Information Position/Rank Assistant Professor in the Tenure Track Department 00246 - Human Genetics City Salt Lake City, UT Track Tenure Track New Position to Begin 07/01/2027 Details The Department of Human Genetics at the University of Utah is continuing a multi-year recruiting initiative for multiple tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant Professor. We invite applications from creative and collaborative scientists who bring innovative questions, approaches, and technologies to genetics and genomics. We are particularly interested in researchers developing or applying cutting-edge and scalable approaches to uncover biological mechanisms and connect genetic variation to function and phenotype. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, technology development; functional genomics and variant effect research; animal, organoid and stem cell models of disease; single-cell and spatial genomics; mechanisms of complex disease; and emerging computational and AI-enabled approaches. We especially value candidates whose work bridges disciplines and complements or connects existing strengths across the department. Researchers pursuing bold and conceptually novel questions in any area of genetics, using human systems, model organisms, or other experimental systems, are strongly encouraged to apply. The Department of Human Genetics is a vibrant and growing research community. We value mentorship and are committed to fostering a collaborative and nurturing community of scientists. Demonstration of a commitment to these values will be important in the selection process. Individuals must have a Ph.D. and/or M.D. or equivalent degrees. Applicants should demonstrate excellence in research with strong potential for securing and sustaining independent extramural funding. Our department and institution offer excellent facilities and resources. Successful candidates will receive a competitive salary, generous startup package, active faculty mentoring, and a research environment that fosters growth and excellence. For full consideration, applications should be received by October 16th, 2026 . Only electronic applications will be accepted, and applications are required to include : Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research Statement Teaching Statement Mentoring and Community Statement Contact details for Recommendations Applicants selected for in-person interviews will be invited to one of two symposia taking place on either Jan 11-12, 2027 or Jan 21-22, 2027. Please mark your calendars accordingly. For questions, please contact Nels Elde – nelde@genetics.utah.edu Quicklink for Posting | https://utah.peopleadmin.com/postings/208129 Values and Culture The University of Utah Health (U of U Health) is a patient focused center distinguished by collaboration, excellence, leadership, and respect. The U of U Health values candidates who are committed to fostering and furthering the culture of compassion, collaboration, innovation, accountability, acceptance, integrity, quality, and trust that is integral to our mission. EEO/Non-Discrimination Information The University of Utah values candidates who have experience working in settings with students and possess a strong commitment to improving access to higher education. Veterans’ preference is extended to qualified applicants, upon request and consistent with University policy and Utah state law. Upon request, reasonable accommodations in the application process will be provided to individuals with disabilities. Consistent with state and federal law, the University of Utah does not discriminate based upon race, ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, sex, sexual orientation, gender, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, pregnancy-related conditions, genetic information, or protected veteran’s status. The University does not discriminate on the basis of sex in the education program or activity that it operates, as required by Title IX and 34 CFR part 106. The requirement not to discriminate in education programs or activities extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about the application of Title IX and its regulations may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, to the Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights, or both. To request a reasonable accommodation for a disability or if you or someone you know has experienced discrimination or sexual misconduct including sexual harassment, you may contact the Director/Title IX Coordinator in the Office of Equal Opportunity and Title IX ( OEO ). More information, including the Director/Title IX Coordinator’s office address, electronic mail address, and telephone number, can be located at https://www.utah.edu/nondiscrimination/ . Online reports may be submitted at https://oeo.utah.edu . Notice The University is a participating employer with Utah Retirement Systems (“URS”). Eligible new hires with prior URS service, may elect to enroll in URS if they make the election before they become eligible for retirement (usually the first day of work). Contact Human Resources at (801) 581-7447 for information. Individuals who previously retired and are receiving monthly retirement benefits from URS are subject to URS’ post-retirement rules and restrictions. Please contact Utah Retirement Systems at (801) 366-7770 or (800) 695-4877 or University Human Resource Management at (801) 581-7447 if you have questions regarding the post-retirement rules. This position may require the successful completion of a criminal background check and/or drug screen. https://publicsafety.utah.edu/safetyreport/ This report includes statistics about criminal offenses, hate crimes, arrests and referrals for disciplinary action, and Violence Against Women Act offenses. They also provide information about safety and security-related services offered by the University of Utah. A paper copy can be obtained by request at the Department of Public Safety located at 1658 East 500 South. Special Instructions for Candidates Special instructions Thank you for your interest in a tenure-track faculty position in the Department of Human Genetics at the University of Utah. Please read the following instructions carefully before preparing your application. Cover Letter (1-2 pages). Please include a cover letter that introduces yourself and your candidacy. Summarize your research accomplishments and the direction you intend to take your independent program. Explain what draws you to this department specifically, and what you would contribute to it. This letter should frame your application rather than duplicate the research statement. Curriculum Vitae. Summarize the key features of your training and any related professional experience, including publications, teaching, mentoring, funding, outreach, presentations, and awards. Research Statement. (3 pages). Describe your most significant prior contributions to science and future plans for your independent research program. The research statement should describe short- and long-term goals and provide detailed plans for accomplishing some of those goals, including suitable external funding mechanisms. Teaching Statement (1 pages). Our department is committed to graduate and medical student education. Describe your commitment to education, your teaching philosophy, and the courses you might teach. Mentoring and Community Statement (1 page). Our department seeks individuals with a strong commitment to and expertise in promoting mentorship and community in academic and research settings. Describe any mentorship and community building efforts you have engaged in, as well as plans for fostering success and overcoming barriers in a research environment. This document should be uploaded as an applicant document labeled “Personal Statement”. Contact details for Recommendations. Provide the names, institutional affiliations, and email addresses of three individuals who are familiar with your work and can evaluate your potential for success as an independent researcher. Referees will be contacted later in the process. This document should be uploaded as an applicant document labeled “List of References”. Open Date 08/31/2026 Close Date 01/11/2027 Open Until Filled Yes Requisition Number PRN04107F Type Faculty Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). One measure of faculty diversity at the School of Medicine is the proportion of faculty members who come from a rural background. Did you graduate high school from a town with less than 10,000 persons? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Statement Research Statement List of References Personal Statement Optional Documents University Human Resource Management 250 East 200 South, Suite 125 Salt Lake City, UT 84111 Contact us: (801) 581-2169 By Email: employment@utah.edu To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
